# Per-profile API keys for services the agent drives.
#
# Copy this file to your profile's state dir as `secrets.env`:
#   cp sandbox_templates/common/secrets.env.template \
#      /Volumes/DataDrive/.claude-colima/profiles/<profile>/secrets.env
# then fill in the keys you need and delete the lines you don't.
#
# `scripts/profile.sh <profile> up` also drops a `secrets.env.example` copy of
# this template into the profile dir for convenience.
#
# SECURITY / MECHANICS
#   * Injected as an OPTIONAL env_file (required: false) into the agent
#     container — the same mechanism as db.env. `ensure_state` chmod 600s it on
#     every `up`, and `wipe` preserves it the way it preserves db.env.
#   * env_file is read only at container CREATE. After editing, recreate:
#       scripts/profile.sh <p> recreate
#     A plain `up` will NOT re-read this file for an already-running agent.
#     This is the single most common way a correct key looks like a wrong one.
#   * The agent never sees this FILE. It is not bind-mounted into /workspace;
#     only the variables surface inside the container. Keep it where it is —
#     under the profile state dir on the drive, outside the repo tree.
#   * Each service's HOST must also be in proxy/allowed_domains.txt, in a tier
#     that is actually open. A key with no allowlist entry fails as a Squid
#     TCP_DENIED/403, which does not look like a missing host unless you read
#     the access log.
#   * VARIABLE NAMES ARE NOT A MATTER OF TASTE. Each one is whatever its
#     consumer actually reads; a plausible-looking synonym fails silently as a
#     missing key. Names below are cited to their call site — check there, not
#     the vendor's docs, before renaming anything.
#   * Keys belong in the environment, never on argv: the Bash tool records
#     argv in the transcript and Squid logs URLs, so a key in either is a leak
#     the agent cannot see or undo.

# --- ClickUp personal API token — for the vendored `myclickup` CLI -----------
# CLICKUP_TOKEN is the canonical name and it is read from the ENVIRONMENT ONLY
# (myclickup src/myclickup/config.py: TOKEN_VAR, resolution order in the module
# docstring). CLICKUP_API_TOKEN is accepted as a DEPRECATED alias.
# CLICKUP_API_KEY is wrong and reads as unset — ClickUp's own docs call this a
# personal API token, never a key. Format: pk_...
#
# Per-account variants CLICKUP_TOKEN_<ACCOUNT> (e.g. CLICKUP_TOKEN_ACME) take
# precedence when CLICKUP_ACCOUNT selects that account.
#
# NEVER put a token in `.myclickup.toml` — that file is for non-secret pins
# (workspace_id, list_id) and myclickup rejects it outright if a top-level key
# contains "token" or "key" (its ADR-0005).
#
# Host: api.clickup.com, ALWAYS ON in proxy/allowed_domains.txt [clickup].
# Not needed for the claude.ai-hosted ClickUp MCP connector, which is OAuth and
# holds its own token — this is the CLI path only.
# CLICKUP_TOKEN=pk_...

# --- Optional, non-secret ClickUp pins ---------------------------------------
# Only worth setting here if a profile works one workspace across many repos.
# The per-repo alternative is a `.myclickup.toml` at the repo root, which is the
# better home for it: it travels with the repo and is visible in review.
# Unset is the LOUD state — myclickup falls back to the token's first workspace
# and warns on every call. A WRONG id is the silent one.
# CLICKUP_WORKSPACE_ID=
# CLICKUP_LIST_ID=

# =============================================================================
# WEB-READ BROKER KEYS — read by `webfetch` (sandbox_templates/bin/webfetch)
# =============================================================================
# The broker is the sanctioned way the agent reads the open web: curl/wget are
# denied, so it fetches THROUGH a hosted reader API whose host is allowlisted
# under [web-read]. All four are PEERS — `webfetch --via <b>` is required and
# has no default, and a failure on one is a reason to try the next, not a
# blocked read. `webfetch backends` reports which keys are actually set in this
# profile. Details: docs/web-read-broker.md.
#
# You do not need all four. One working backend makes the tool useful; the rest
# are redundancy against a quota wall or an outage.

# --- Tavily — search (synthesized answer) + clean extract --------------------
# Read by `webfetch --via tavily`. Host api.tavily.com, live under [web-read].
# Get a key at https://app.tavily.com/ (free tier available). Format: tvly-...
# TAVILY_API_KEY=tvly-...

# --- TinyFish — search (snippets) + markdown extract, PDFs -------------------
# Read by `webfetch --via tinyfish`. Hosts api.search/api.fetch.tinyfish.ai.
# Search and Fetch are the vendor's free tier.
#
# THE SAME KEY also unlocks their paid Agent and Browser APIs — a cloud browser
# the caller steers, which is a WRITE surface, not a read one. The broker never
# calls those hosts, they are deliberately absent from the allowlist, and
# webfetch.test.sh asserts both facts. Do not connect the TinyFish MCP inside a
# profile to reach them.
# Get a key at https://agent.tinyfish.ai/api-keys  Format: sk-tinyfish-...
# TINYFISH_API_KEY=sk-tinyfish-...

# --- Jina Reader — single-URL clean-markdown read ---------------------------
# Read by `webfetch --via jina`. Hosts r.jina.ai (extract) + s.jina.ai (search).
# OPTIONAL in the real sense: keyless works and is rate-limited; a key raises the
# limit. This is the one backend that does something without an entry here.
# Format: jina_...
# JINA_API_KEY=jina_...

# --- Firecrawl — JS-heavy pages, PDFs (extract only) ------------------------
# Read by `webfetch --via firecrawl`. Host api.firecrawl.dev — note .dev, NOT
# .com; the wrong one is a TCP_DENIED that reads like a bad key.
# Format: fc-...
# FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=fc-...

# --- Adding a key for something else -----------------------------------------
# 1. Find the exact variable name in the consumer's source, not its docs.
# 2. Add its host to proxy/allowed_domains.txt in the right tier, with a
#    one-line justification above the block (CLAUDE.md checklist).
# 3. Add the variable here with a comment citing where the name came from.
# 4. `scripts/profile.sh <p> recreate` — not `up`.
# docs/numerai-profile-seed.md walks the whole shape end to end for a
# submit-only Numerai key, including how to prove the key is not over-scoped.

# =============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY
# =============================================================================
# windows-ai-sandbox's template also lists HF_TOKEN and FAL_KEY. Both belong to
# the GPU/ML stack (ComfyUI, fal) that is deliberately out of scope here
# (work/0001 §0.2), and neither has a reader in this image.
#
# A key whose consumer does not exist is worse than an absent one: it reads as
# configured, and the failure surfaces as a puzzling silence rather than a
# missing-key error. So if either tool is ever ported, bring its key block over
# WITH its reader and WITH its host in the allowlist, in the same change — which
# is exactly how the four web-read keys above arrived (work/0005), after being
# held back for one release for precisely this reason.
